# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — Supabase (D6)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two connection strings, and they are NOT interchangeable. Both are Postgres
# URLs — neither is the project API URL (https://<ref>.supabase.co).
#
#   DATABASE_URL  transaction-mode pooler, port 6543 — every application query.
#                 postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<pw>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres
#                 Note the username is postgres.<ref>, not postgres.
#   DIRECT_URL    session mode, port 5432 — migrations only. A pooled connection
#                 cannot run DDL, so `pnpm db:migrate` fails against 6543.
#
# Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → Database → Connection string.
# Until DATABASE_URL is set, DB-backed endpoints return 500 by design.
DATABASE_URL=
DIRECT_URL=

# Sockets per instance. Raise only with the pooler's own limit in mind.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase Auth (Phase A) — omit until migrated; auth screens show
# "configuration required" rather than a broken form.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Server-side ONLY. Bypasses Row Level Security entirely — putting this in a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable is a full database compromise (docs/BACKEND.md).
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hosting / routing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Apex domain that storefront subdomains hang off: {slug}.{ROOT_DOMAIN}.
# Read by proxy.ts and lib/api.ts — storefront URLs are wrong without it.
#
# Override to `localhost` when running pnpm test:integration (see tests/README.md).
# Download URLs are generated server-side from this, so leaving it as markii.shop
# points the digital-delivery suite at a host that does not resolve locally — or,
# on a machine with wildcard DNS, at production.
ROOT_DOMAIN=markii.shop

# Overrides the platform's own base URL. Inferred from VERCEL_* when unset.
# Leave BLANK in .env.local — setting it to the production domain makes local
# previews and generated storefront URLs point at production.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Uploads & digital delivery (§18.8)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No variables of its own: storage uses NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above (D6 task 8, done).
#
# Two buckets, created by `pnpm storage:init` — they are NOT part of the
# migration chain, because Supabase creates buckets through its Storage API
# rather than DDL, so a fresh project has none and uploads fail until you run it:
#
#   public-media    public   product images, referenced from storefront HTML
#   digital-assets  PRIVATE  the files merchants sell, reachable only via a
#                            short-lived signed URL minted per paid download
#
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN is no longer read. Vercel Blob and the local
# public/uploads fallback are both gone — the fallback silently broke every
# deployment, since Vercel's filesystem is ephemeral.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email — split by whose mail it is (G1). Never cross the streams.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resend: Markii's OWN mail only, from markii.shop — contact form, support,
# staff auth, invoices, dunning. Never merchant mail.
# The EMAIL_FROM domain must be verified on the Resend account or every send
# 403s; `markii.shop` is verified, `markii.shop.com` is not a thing.
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM=Markii <support@markii.shop>
# Optional inbox for the marketing contact form (defaults to support@markii.shop)
CONTACT_TO=support@markii.shop

# AWS SES: everything sent ON MERCHANTS' BEHALF, from their own verified
# domains — order confirmations, shipping and refund notices, digital delivery.
#
# The transport is built (lib/email/ses.ts, SigV4 over the SES v2 API). What is
# missing is account setup, and three of the four steps have lead time:
#
#   1. These three variables.
#   2. SANDBOX ESCAPE — an AWS support request with a queue in front of it.
#      Until granted, SES accepts mail only to addresses you have verified, so
#      sending will sign correctly and AWS will still refuse most of it.
#      START THIS EARLY; it is not instant and it is refusable.
#   3. A configuration set with an SNS destination pointed at
#      /api/webhooks/ses, so bounces and complaints reach the suppression list.
#      Without it SES still sends, nothing is ever suppressed, and the account
#      drifts toward a bounce-rate suspension unseen.
#   4. Per-merchant domain verification — a product feature, not setup. Each
#      merchant verifies their own domain in Settings → Email.
#
# Merchant mail NEVER falls back to Resend. A merchant's order confirmation
# leaving from markii.shop would put their bounces on Markii's sending
# reputation, which is the whole reason the two streams exist (G1).
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# Only for temporary credentials (STS / instance roles). Leave blank otherwise.
AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=

# Name of the SES configuration set that publishes bounce and complaint events
# to SNS. Sending works without it; suppression does not (step 3 above).
SES_CONFIGURATION_SET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Payments — x402 rail
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BASE_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL=

# Demo escape hatch: accepts checkout without verifying settlement on-chain.
# Never set this in production.
DEMO_SKIP_PAYMENT_VERIFICATION=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Payments — Stripe (§17 billing, §18.4 card rail)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO SEPARATE CONCERNS ON ONE ACCOUNT, and they fail differently:
#
#   Connect Standard  merchants take card payments from shoppers. Markii is
#                     NEVER in the funds flow and takes NO application_fee_amount
#                     — merchants keep their own Stripe account and their own
#                     negotiated rates (D4). This is what makes the public claim
#                     "your Stripe account, your rates" literally true.
#   Platform billing  subscriptions and threshold fees charged TO merchants, on
#                     Markii's own invoice. Here Markii is the merchant of
#                     record. Revenue never touches the merchant's payment flow.
#
# Setup, in the order that wastes least time. Step 2 has a queue in front of it:
#
#   1. Create the platform Stripe account. Test mode is enough to build against.
#   2. ENABLE CONNECT, TYPE STANDARD, and complete the platform profile. Stripe
#      reviews it before granting live-mode Connect, and it is refusable. Start
#      this early — same shape of problem as the SES sandbox escape above.
#   3. Set the OAuth redirect URI for Standard onboarding (merchants are
#      redirected to Stripe and back). Needs a real URL on your domain.
#   4. Create Products/Prices matching the plan catalog in lib/plans.ts and
#      collect their price ids — no subscription can be created without them.
#   5. Add TWO webhook endpoints, both pointing at /api/webhooks/stripe: one for
#      platform events, one with "Listen to events on Connected accounts"
#      checked. They are separate endpoints with SEPARATE SIGNING SECRETS, and
#      the route refuses to fall back from one to the other — sharing a secret
#      would make an unverifiable event look verified.
#
# Locally, skip step 5 with the Stripe CLI, which prints its own secret:
#   stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/webhooks/stripe
#
# The only variable read today is STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, and only as a "configured"
# signal (lib/payments/index.ts). The card rail, subscriptions, invoices, and
# payment methods are NOT implemented — they refuse unconditionally, so setting
# a key does not switch them on. It unblocks the work; it is not the work.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# Stripe-hosted Elements only. Card data must never reach a Markii origin —
# that is what keeps PCI scope at SAQ-A (docs/BACKEND.md).
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Signing secrets, one per endpoint from step 5. whsec_…
# Without the matching one, /api/webhooks/stripe answers 503 rather than 200:
# acknowledging an event it cannot verify would stop Stripe retrying and discard
# every event for the whole window the secret was missing.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
STRIPE_CONNECT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Connect Standard OAuth client id (Stripe Dashboard → Connect → Settings →
# "Integration"; starts `ca_`). Without it merchants cannot connect their Stripe
# account at all, and /api/integrations/stripe/connect answers 503.
#
# Register the OAuth redirect URI in the same Stripe screen, exactly:
#   {NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL or your production URL}/api/integrations/stripe/callback
# Stripe rejects any redirect_uri that is not registered, so a mismatch here
# fails at the moment a merchant tries to connect, not at deploy time.
#
# Markii NEVER asks for or stores a merchant secret key. The merchant authorises
# through OAuth, keeps their own account, rates, dashboard, and payouts, and
# Markii acts on the account with its own key plus `Stripe-Account` (D4).
STRIPE_CONNECT_CLIENT_ID=
